## Context

A digital learning platform built for the Cimory Group and Kanzler sales teams that evolved into a broader company-wide Learning Management System (LMS) initiative handed over to IT with full technical documentation.

## Problem

Training materials for distributed sales teams needed a structured delivery channel where HR could update learning content without engineering bottlenecks, verify that videos were actually watched, and measure comprehension consistently across teams.

## My role

Owner of initial build: I translated HR and sales-training needs into functional requirements, designed and built the platform, ran performance engineering benchmarks, and prepared the handover documentation (`HANDOVER.md`, system status, performance report, and feature specs) for the IT team's production deployment.

## As-is analysis

Sales enablement relied on manual training coordination and static material sharing without automated completion tracking, quiz scoring, or unified analytics for leadership.

## Requirements

- Video learning modules with anti-skip viewing-progress validation, chapter quizzes, flashcards, XP, badges, and leaderboards.
- An admin CMS with Excel bulk import so HR can manage courses and content without code changes.
- Security controls including JWT authentication, bcrypt password hashing, rate limiting, Content Security Policy (CSP), and screen-capture restriction mechanisms.
- Permission-based Power BI reporting and verified performance under high concurrent load.

## Solution design

The backend started on Express.js. As the scope grew toward a company-wide LMS, I introduced a hybrid Express.js and NestJS architecture using the Strangler Pattern (reaching ~40% migration to NestJS with Prisma 6 and PostgreSQL 15+) so new features could ship cleanly without rewriting legacy routes all at once. Learning videos are stored in Cloudflare R2 object storage, decoupled from the application server.

## Build

Built with Node.js 20, Express 4, NestJS 10, Prisma 6, PostgreSQL 15+, Vanilla JavaScript (ES2022), Cloudflare R2, JWT authentication, and permission-based Power BI reporting. Demo: `cimory-academy-demo.onrender.com`. Source code and handover docs: [github.com/thunderclouds863/cimory-academy](https://github.com/thunderclouds863/cimory-academy).

## Verification

Executed seven k6 performance engineering scenarios: smoke, load, stress, spike, soak, database benchmarking, and object storage benchmarking. All 7 performance tests passed, including 0.17% error rate at 1,000 virtual users (stress), 0.01% error rate over a 1-hour soak test, and 5,216 transactions per second (TPS) on the PostgreSQL benchmark with zero failed transactions.

## Outcome

Delivered a production-ready LMS foundation validated under 1,000 concurrent virtual users and handed over the complete codebase and documentation to IT for company-wide rollout.

## Reflection

Using the Strangler Pattern allowed the platform to evolve from a fast HR prototype into a structured NestJS architecture without halting delivery, while early k6 load testing surfaced and resolved performance bottlenecks before IT handover.
